From: WhaleOS <whaleos-dev@navercorp.com>
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] config_base: bind-mount /proc/sys/kernel read-only into container

The Waydroid Android image's /system/etc/init/hw/init.rc writes to
/proc/sys/kernel/modprobe (an empty newline) on container boot as a
"disable kernel-side module auto-load" hardening step inherited from
AOSP.

But /proc/sys/kernel/* is NOT a namespaced sysctl on Linux: writes
from inside the LXC container land directly on the host kernel's
global modprobe_path[].  On WhaleOS this is fatal because:

  - CONFIG_STATIC_USERMODEHELPER=y forces every kernel-triggered
    modprobe through /sbin/usermode-helper.
  - /sbin/usermode-helper validates argv[0] against a small whitelist
    (/sbin/modprobe, /usr/bin/modprobe, ...).  Any other value
    (including an empty path) triggers LOG(FATAL) -> SIGABRT.
  - Once Android init has cleared modprobe_path on the host, host-side
    mount(2) calls that need lazy module load -- vfat / nls_cp437 /
    nls_iso8859-1 needed by chromeos-install reading the FAT32 EFI
    partition -- cannot trigger module auto-load any more, so
    chromeos-install fails on a fresh USB boot with
    "FAT-fs (sda12): codepage cp437 not found".

We block the leak at the container boundary instead of patching the
Android image: bind-mount the host's /proc/sys/kernel into the
container as read-only.  Android init's defensive `write
/proc/sys/kernel/modprobe` then fails with EROFS, which is non-fatal
inside init.cpp's do_write() (logs an error and continues).  No
Waydroid runtime functionality depends on being able to override
host kernel sysctls.

This is a WhaleOS-specific patch: regular Linux hosts neither enforce
a strict usermode-helper whitelist nor rely on lazy module auto-load
during installer boot, so the same sysctl leak goes unnoticed there.

The `optional` flag is defensive -- if for some reason the host
/proc/sys/kernel is not yet visible at container start, container boot
proceeds without the isolation plug rather than aborting.  (In
practice the preceding `lxc.mount.entry = proc proc proc ...` ensures
procfs is mounted first, so the bind always succeeds.)

--- a/data/configs/config_base
+++ b/data/configs/config_base
@@ -18,3 +18,7 @@ lxc.include = /var/lib/waydroid/lxc/waydroid/config_session

 lxc.mount.entry = proc proc proc nosuid,nodev,noexec,create=dir 0 0
 lxc.mount.entry = sysfs sys sysfs nosuid,nodev,noexec,ro,create=dir 0 0
+
+# WhaleOS: block container writes to host /proc/sys/kernel/* (modprobe path,
+# core_pattern, hotplug, ...).  See PATCH 12/12 header for full rationale.
+lxc.mount.entry = /proc/sys/kernel proc/sys/kernel none ro,bind,optional 0 0
